# Desktop entry for satl, which opens its own console (GTK_AND_NO_DEPENDENCIES.md
# GTK-17). Until 2026-09-22 this entry started satl-term, a second binary that
# ran satl in a terminal; the author removed it that day -- "we are getting rid
# of satl-term and replacing it with something built in to the satl exe" -- and
# satl draws the same window itself, in the same process, so the exit status of
# the program is satl's own.
#
# The file name is the app id satl gives its own console, and it has to be:
# console_launch.cpp sets prgname to org.satellite.terminal before GTK starts,
# GDK hands that to the compositor as the Wayland app_id, and a desktop shell
# then looks for <that id>.desktop. Named anything else, the window arrives
# associated with no launcher: no icon in the switcher, nothing to pin.
# StartupWMClass says the same thing a second time for shells that match on
# WM_CLASS. The id keeps satl-term's name on purpose: it is the id installs,
# pins and the .satl association already carry.
#
# --console AND NOT A BARE satl, although a bare satl opens its console too when
# nothing gave it one: a launcher that hands satl a pipe or a file for its
# output would be read as "somebody is reading this", and the window would not
# open. The flag says what is meant.
#
# Exec is the bare command name rather than an absolute path, because the prefix
# is not known when this file is written and a hard-coded /usr/local would be
# wrong in every distribution package. TryExec makes the entry hide itself if
# the binary is not on PATH, which is the correct behaviour for a menu.
#
# %f is honest because every launch is its own satl process: there is no
# single-instance application to hand a second file to and drop it. With no file
# -- the menu entry -- `satl --console` is the prompt in a console.
#
# "Satellite 004" AND NOT "Satellite" SINCE 2026-09-23, when the author asked for
# 003 back beside it -- "a button for 003 and a button for 004 on my machine, as
# some programs will run in 003, some in 004". 003's is
# org.satellite.terminal003.desktop, "Satellite 003", running satl-term from
# ~/.satl-003; 003's window took the new id so this one could keep its own.
#
# Icon is the application id rather than a file path, because that is how the
# icon theme spec is looked up: the shell searches every theme directory for
# <name> with any extension it understands, so the id names the artwork we
# install under share/icons/hicolor/. It matches the file name and
# StartupWMClass for the same reason all three agree -- a shell that finds the
# launcher by any one of them finds the icon too.
#
# MimeType is what makes this entry the handler for a .satl file: the type is
# declared in dist/application-x-satellite.xml, and a file manager offers the
# programs whose entries name it here. Without this line the type is still
# recognised and still gets its own icon, but opening a file has nothing to
# open it with.
#
# %f in Exec above is what carries the file through, and is already correct for
# this: it is a single-file placeholder, which matches a handler launched once
# per file. It is NOT upgraded to %F -- satl runs one program in one console,
# so a multi-file selection has to arrive as several launches rather than as
# one launch that would silently drop every path but the first.
[Desktop Entry]
Type=Application
Version=1.0
Name=Satellite 004
GenericName=Satellite Terminal
Comment=Interactive terminal for the satellite programming language, version 004
Exec=satl --console %f
TryExec=satl
Icon=org.satellite.terminal
Terminal=false
Categories=Development;IDE;
Keywords=satellite;interpreter;repl;language;programming;
MimeType=application/x-satellite;
StartupNotify=true
StartupWMClass=org.satellite.terminal
